169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (The Number Guessing Game is an interactive, console-based application developed in Python 3 as the first-semester project for the course Programming Fundamentals and Logic Building Using Python \(Problem Statement No. 89\). The computer selects a secret integer between 1 and 100 and the player must deduce it from directional feedback \("Too high!" or "Too low!"\) given after every incorrect guess.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Problem addressed: the project demonstrates how loops, conditional branching, functions and exception handling can be combined to build a complete, robust program that responds correctly to normal, boundary and invalid user input.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Approach and tools: the program is written in pure Python 3 using only the standard random module. It is organised into four functions \(show_guidelines, generate_secret_number, play_game and main\) and extends the base requirement into a progressive five-level game with five shared lifelines, input validation, a deterministic TEST_MODE switch and a session high-score tracker.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Current progress and expected outcome: the program is fully implemented and all mandatory test cases from Problem Statement No. 89 have passed. The final outcome is a reliable game that never crashes on invalid input and can be switched between deterministic testing and randomised play by changing a single constant.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 94 0 R
/T (2 Introduction)
/E (2 Introduction)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Number guessing is one of the classic beginner programs in computer science because it exercises the three basic building blocks of every program: sequence, selection and iteration. At the same time it introduces user interaction, data validation and state management in a problem that is small enough to be understood completely.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Python is an excellent language for problem solving at this level. Its readable syntax lets a student concentrate on logic rather than language details, its built-in int\(\) conversion with try-except makes input validation concise, and its standard library \(for example random\) removes the need for external tools. Python is also widely used in AI and Machine Learning, which makes it the natural first language for this programme.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Problem statement. Develop a console program in which the computer chooses a secret number between 1 and 100 and the player has a limited number of attempts \(5\) to guess it. After each wrong guess the program must say whether the guess was too high or too low. The program must handle normal inputs, boundary inputs \(1 and 100\), invalid inputs \(non-numeric and out-of-range values\) and the case where all attempts are exhausted.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Motivation. The base problem was extended to make it a more complete piece of software engineering practice: carrying lifelines across levels creates meaningful state to track, a global high-score variable shows how module-level state works, and the TEST_MODE flag shows how randomised programs can be made testable. The project therefore reinforces functions, loops,)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
